<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>quantity-of-heat</title></head><body><h1>quantity-of-heat</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Heat (H) - Mass and Temperature Dependency</li><li>Newton's Law of Cooling</li><li>Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids and liquids</li><li>Specific Heat Capacity</li><li>Change of Phases and Latent Heat</li><li>Heat Capacity (Thermal Capacity, C')</li><li>Specific latent heat of fusion and vaporization</li><li>Triple Point</li><li>Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion and vaporiz…</li><li>Principle of Calorimetry (Method of Mixtures)</li><li>Newton's Law of Cooling - Statement</li><li>Triple point</li><li>Finding an Unknown Specific Heat Capacity by Comparing Cooling Rates</li><li>Limitations of Newton's Law of Cooling</li></ul><h2>Heat (H) - Mass and Temperature Dependency</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Heat is energy in transit:** Heat ($H$) is the thermal energy transferred between systems due to a temperature difference. It is NOT a property of a body - you cannot say "a body contains heat." A body contains internal energy; heat is the transfer of that energy.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Mass dependency (H ∝ m):** For a given substance and temperature change, the heat required is directly proportional to the mass: $H \propto m$. Doubling the mass doubles the heat needed. This is because more molecules means more total kinetic energy must change.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Temperature dependency (H ∝ Δθ):** For a given mass, heat is directly proportional to the temperature change: $H \propto \Delta\theta$, where $\Delta\theta = \theta_f - \theta_i$ (final − initial). Heating water from 20°C to 80°C requires 3× the heat of heating from 20°C to 40°C.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Combined relation:** Combining both: $H \propto m \cdot \Delta\theta$. Introducing the constant of proportionality - the specific heat capacity ($c$) - gives the fundamental equation: **$H = m \cdot c \cdot \Delta\theta$**</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Sign convention:** When heat is absorbed (temperature rises), $H > 0$ and $\Delta\theta > 0$. When heat is released (temperature falls), $H < 0$ and $\Delta\theta < 0$. The sign tells you the direction of energy flow.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Units of heat:** SI unit is the **joule (J)**. The CGS unit is the **calorie (cal)**. Conversion: $1\text{ cal} = 4.184\text{ J}$ (thermochemical calorie). Another unit, the BTU (British Thermal Unit), is used in engineering: $1\text{ BTU} \approx 1055\text{ J}$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Heat vs. Temperature:** Temperature ($\theta$) is an intensive property - it doesn't depend on the amount of substance. Heat ($H$) is extensive - it depends on mass. A cup of water at 80°C has a higher temperature than a bathtub at 50°C, but the bathtub has more thermal energy (and more heat to transfer if cooled).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Heat vs. Internal Energy:** Internal energy ($U$) is the total energy stored in a system's molecules. Heat ($H$) is the energy TRANSFERRED. When you heat a body, its internal energy increases: $\Delta U = H$ (at constant volume, no work done).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Real-world analogy:** Think of water flowing from a high tank to a low tank. Temperature is like water level (height), heat is like the amount of water that flows. Two tanks at the same height have the same temperature, but the larger tank has more water (more heat capacity).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Calorimetry context:** In all calorimetry problems, the equation $H = mc\Delta\theta$ is applied to EACH substance separately. Hot body: $H_{lost} = m_h c_h (\theta_h - \theta_{eq})$. Cold body: $H_{gained} = m_c c_c (\theta_{eq} - \theta_c)$. Set $H_{lost} = H_{gained}$.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Common values of c (for reference):** Water: $c = 4200\text{ J/kg·K}$. Ice: $c = 2100\text{ J/kg·K}$. Copper: $c = 390\text{ J/kg·K}$. Aluminum: $c = 900\text{ J/kg·K}$. Iron: $c = 450\text{ J/kg·K}$. Lead: $c = 130\text{ J/kg·K}$. Higher $c$ means more heat needed per degree.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Why water is special:** Water has the highest specific heat among common liquids ($4200\text{ J/kg·K}$). This means it absorbs huge amounts of heat with minimal temperature change - why it's used as a coolant in car radiators, power plants, and why coastal climates are moderate.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Quick estimation rule:** For a 1 kg mass of water, raising temperature by 1°C requires 4200 J. So heating 2 kg by 50°C requires $H = 2 \times 4200 \times 50 = 420{,}000\text{ J} = 420\text{ kJ}$. This is roughly the energy in 100 g of sugar.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Dimensional check:** $[H] = [M][L]^2[T]^{-2}$ (energy). $[mc\Delta\theta] = [M] \cdot [L]^2[T]^{-2}[\Theta]^{-1} \cdot [\Theta] = [M][L]^2[T]^{-2}$. ✓ Dimensions match.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Power and heat rate:** If heat is supplied at a constant rate (power $P$), then $H = Pt$. So $Pt = mc\Delta\theta$, which gives $\Delta\theta/t = P/(mc)$. A 1500 W kettle heats 1 kg of water at $\Delta\theta/t = 1500/(1 \times 4200) = 0.357\text{ °C/s}$.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Connection of Heat (H) — Mass and Temperature Dependency to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat and temperature are the same thing. ✅ Temperature is the average molecular kinetic energy (intensive). Heat is the total energy transferred (extensive). A spark at 2000°C has tiny heat content; a bathtub at 40°C has huge heat content.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A body "contains" heat. ✅ A body contains internal energy (kinetic + potential of its molecules). Heat is the TRANSFER of energy due to temperature difference. Once energy is in the body, it's no longer "heat." Think of it like "payment" - once received, it's no longer a payment; it's just money in your account.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All materials heat up at the same rate with the same power. ✅ Different materials have different specific heats. 1 kW heats 1 kg of aluminum ($c=900$) much faster than 1 kg of water ($c=4200$). The aluminum's temperature rises ~4.7× faster.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The formula H = mcΔθ works for phase changes too. ✅ This formula ONLY works when temperature changes WITHOUT a phase change. During melting (ice → water at 0°C) or boiling (water → steam at 100°C), temperature stays constant - you need $H = mL$ instead.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Specific heat capacity depends on the amount of substance. ✅ Specific heat capacity is an INTENSIVE property - it depends only on the MATERIAL, not the mass. 1 kg of copper has $c = 390\text{ J/kg·K}$, same as 1 g of copper.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat always flows from hot to cold, so a cold object can never lose heat to a hot one. ✅ Heat naturally flows from hot to cold. However, a refrigerator CAN move heat from cold to hot - but only by doing external work (compressor). This doesn't violate the second law.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ If you add heat, temperature always rises. ✅ During a phase change (melting, boiling, sublimation), added heat goes into breaking intermolecular bonds (increasing potential energy), NOT increasing kinetic energy. Temperature stays constant during the transition.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Heat (H) — Mass and Temperature Dependency covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Newton's Law of Cooling</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Purpose of Newton's law of cooling:** The law provides a simple mathematical model to predict how the temperature of a body changes with time when it is placed in a surroundings of constant temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Forensic science — estimating time of death:** A corpse cools according to Newton's law after death (the "algor mortis" process). If body temperature is measured at time $t$, and the ambient temperature is known, the law estimates how long ago death occurred: $\theta(t) = \theta_0 + (\theta_{body} - \theta_0)e^{-K't}$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Engineering — cooling system design:** Engineers use Newton's law to size radiators, heat sinks, and cooling fans. For a component dissipating power $P$, the steady-state temperature rise is $\Delta T = P/(kA)$, where $k$ is the heat transfer coefficient.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Food industry — pasteurization and chilling:** After pasteurization, milk must be cooled to below 4°C within a specified time to prevent bacterial growth. Newton's law calculates the required cooling time and the size of the heat exchanger.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Everyday life — coffee and tea:** A hot cup of coffee cools faster when the room is cold and slower when the room is warm. The law explains why adding cold milk immediately (rather than waiting) keeps coffee hotter longer — the milk lowers the initial temperature, reducing the rate of heat loss.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Metallurgy — quenching and annealing:** The rate of cooling a hot metal determines its microstructure and hardness. Quenching in water (large $k$) produces hard martensite; slow cooling in oil or air (smaller $k$) produces softer phases. Newton's law approximates the cooling curve.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Climate and building science — thermal mass:** Walls and floors with high heat capacity (large $mc$) change temperature slowly. Newton's law predicts how quickly a building cools after the heating is turned off, helping design energy-efficient HVAC systems.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Weather forecasting — radiation fog:** On clear nights, the ground loses heat by radiation. Newton's law approximates how quickly surface temperature drops. When it falls below the dew point, fog forms. This is used in agricultural frost warnings.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Electronic devices — CPU thermal management:** A processor generates heat at rate $P$. Without cooling, its temperature rises until heat loss balances power input. With a heatsink, Newton's law predicts the equilibrium temperature: $T_{eq} = T_{ambient} + P/k$.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Calorimetry corrections — radiation loss:** In precision calorimetry, heat loss to surroundings causes systematic errors. Newton's law allows correction: the heat lost during an experiment is $\int k(\theta - \theta_0)\,dt$, which can be estimated from the cooling curve.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Medicine — therapeutic hypothermia:** After cardiac arrest, patients are cooled to 32–34°C to reduce brain damage. Newton's law guides the cooling protocol, predicting how long ice packs or cooling blankets must be applied.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Photography — darkroom processing:** Chemical baths in photography must be kept at a precise temperature. Newton's law predicts how quickly a fresh bath reaches the target temperature when moved from a warm storage area.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Automotive — engine warm-up and cooling:** A car engine reaches operating temperature faster in cold weather (larger $\theta - \theta_0$) but the cooling system must dissipate more heat. Newton's law models both warm-up and cooling phases.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**NEB exam applications:** Identify real-world situations where Newton's law applies. Explain how the law is used in forensic time-of-death estimation and in finding unknown specific heats by the method of cooling.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Limitation reminder:** Newton's law is an approximation. Real cooling involves radiation ($\propto T^4 - T_0^4$), convection, and conduction simultaneously. The law is most accurate when temperature differences are moderate (20–50°C) and convection dominates.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Newton's Law of Cooling:** This topic covers the fundamental concepts of newton's law of cooling in the physics curriculum.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Understanding newton's law of cooling is essential for solving problems and building higher-level mathematical skills.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Key definitions and theorems related to newton's law of cooling should be memorized and practiced regularly.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Newton's law of cooling tells you the exact time of death. ✅ It gives an ESTIMATE. The model ignores many factors (clothing, body size, air movement, humidity) that affect real cooling rates.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The law works equally well for very large temperature differences (e.g., a red-hot iron in air). ✅ For large $\Delta T$, radiation ($\propto T^4$) dominates over convection, and Newton's linear law becomes inaccurate.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A larger object always cools faster. ✅ Larger objects have larger heat capacity ($mc$), which slows cooling. The cooling RATE depends on the ratio $k/(mc)$, not just on size.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Newton's law of cooling is the same as the Stefan-Boltzmann law. ✅ Newton's law is linear ($d\theta/dt \propto \theta - \theta_0$). Stefan-Boltzmann is quartic ($\propto T^4 - T_0^4$). Newton's law is an approximation valid for small $\Delta T$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The cooling constant $k$ is a property of the material. ✅ $k$ depends on the SURFACE properties (area, texture, air flow), not the material inside. Two objects of the same material but different shapes have different $k$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Once a body reaches room temperature, it stays there forever. ✅ It reaches thermal equilibrium asymptotically — it gets arbitrarily close but never exactly reaches $\theta_0$ in finite time (though for practical purposes it appears to).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The method of cooling (comparing cooling rates) gives the exact specific heat. ✅ It gives an approximate value. Errors arise from non-identical conditions, radiation losses, and the approximation that $k$ is constant.</td></tr><tr><td>Carefully distinguish between similar-looking concepts in newton's law of cooling.</td></tr><tr><td>Pay attention to the conditions under which formulas and theorems apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice with multiple examples to solidify your understanding.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Newton's Law of Cooling is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids and liquids</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids and liquids — Quantity of Heat, Physics (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: measurement of specific heat capacity of solids, liquids · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** Heat Q = mcΔT; specific heat c, thermal capacity C = mc, heat capacity per mole = C. molar.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** Latent heat L: Q = mL (change of state at constant T). Calorimetry: heat lost = heat gained.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** Newton's law of cooling: rate of heat loss ∝ (T − T₀); dθ/dt = −k(θ − θ₀).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Liquids.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids</td></tr><tr><td>• Liquids</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids" as one blob → ✅ split into: measurement of specific heat capacity of solids, liquids.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ heat q = mcδt; specific heat c, thermal capacity c = mc, heat capacity per mole = c. molar..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids and liquids (Quantity of Heat, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids, liquids. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Specific Heat Capacity</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Specific Heat Capacity:** This topic covers the fundamental concepts of specific heat capacity in the physics curriculum.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Understanding specific heat capacity is essential for solving problems and building higher-level mathematical skills.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Key definitions and theorems related to specific heat capacity should be memorized and practiced regularly.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Definition:** Specific heat capacity ($c$) is the amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of **1 kg** (SI) or **1 g** (CGS) of a substance by **1 K** (or 1°C). It is an intrinsic property of the material.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Formula:** From $H = mc\Delta\theta$, solving for $c$: **$c = \frac{H}{m \cdot \Delta\theta}$**. This shows $c$ is the heat per unit mass per unit temperature change.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Water - the benchmark:** $c_{water} = 4186\text{ J/kg·K}$ (more precisely 4181.3 J/kg·K at 15°C, but 4200 J/kg·K is standard for exam work). This is unusually high compared to most substances, making water an excellent thermal buffer.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Ice vs. liquid water:** $c_{ice} \approx 2100\text{ J/kg·K}$ - about HALF of liquid water's specific heat. This means ice heats up twice as fast as liquid water for the same mass. During melting, the $c$ changes discontinuously.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Metals - low specific heats:** Copper: $c = 390\text{ J/kg·K}$, Aluminum: $c = 900\text{ J/kg·K}$, Iron: $c = 450\text{ J/kg·K}$, Lead: $c = 130\text{ J/kg·K}$, Gold: $c = 129\text{ J/kg·K}$. Low $c$ means metals respond quickly to heating - useful for cookware, bad for thermal storage.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Solids (Dulong-Petit Law):** For most solid elements at room temperature, $c \times M \approx 25\text{ J/mol·K}$ (molar heat capacity). So $c \approx 25/M$ where $M$ is molar mass in kg/mol. Heavy atoms → low $c$ per kg; light atoms → high $c$ per kg.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Liquids:** Water: 4186, Ethanol: 2440, Mercury: 140, Glycerin: 2430, Ammonia (liquid): 4700. Gases have two specific heats ($c_p$ and $c_v$); most liquids have only one since they don't expand significantly.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Gases - two specific heats:** For gases, $c_p > c_v$ always. At constant pressure, gas expands and does work, needing MORE heat for the same temperature rise. For air: $c_p = 1005\text{ J/kg·K}$, $c_v = 718\text{ J/kg·K}$. Ratio $\gamma = c_p/c_v \approx 1.4$.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Temperature dependence:** Specific heat capacity is NOT truly constant - it varies with temperature. For water, $c$ changes from 4217 J/kg·K at 0°C to 4181 J/kg·K at 15°C to 4219 J/kg·K at 100°C. For most school problems, treat $c$ as constant.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Molar heat capacity vs. specific heat:** Molar heat capacity $C_m = c \times M$ (where $M$ = molar mass). For water: $C_m = 4186 \times 0.018 = 75.3\text{ J/mol·K}$. This is useful for comparing substances on a per-molecule basis.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Why specific heat differs:** The specific heat depends on how energy is distributed among molecular degrees of freedom. Monatomic gases store energy only in translation (3 DOF). Diatomic gases also store in rotation (2 DOF). Polyatomic molecules add vibration. More ways to store energy → higher $c$.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Water's anomalous value:** Water's $c = 4186$ is about 5× that of copper and 10× that of lead. This is because water molecules form hydrogen bonds that must be partially broken before molecules can move faster (higher temperature). This "hidden" energy cost makes $c$ high.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Practical - mixing two substances:** When a hot substance (mass $m_1$, $c_1$, $T_1$) is mixed with a cold substance ($m_2$, $c_2$, $T_2$), the equilibrium temperature is: $T_{eq} = \frac{m_1 c_1 T_1 + m_2 c_2 T_2}{m_1 c_1 + m_2 c_2}$. This is a weighted average by heat capacity.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>**NEB focus:** Know the formula $H = mc\Delta\theta$, the values of $c$ for water and common metals, the distinction between $c$ and heat capacity ($C = mc$), and unit conversion between J/kg·K and cal/g·°C ($1\text{ J/kg·K} = 0.000239\text{ cal/g·°C}$).</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>**Common misconception:** "Specific" means "per unit mass" - it does NOT mean "typical" or "usual." Specific heat capacity is a very specific property that varies enormously between materials. Gold ($c=129$) and water ($c=4186$) differ by a factor of 32.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>**SI unit:** $\text{J} \cdot \text{kg}^{-1} \cdot \text{K}^{-1}$ (joule per kilogram per kelvin). Since a temperature INTERVAL of 1 K equals 1°C, this is also written as $\text{J} \cdot \text{kg}^{-1} \cdot \text{°C}^{-1}$.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>**CGS unit:** $\text{cal} \cdot \text{g}^{-1} \cdot \text{°C}^{-1}$ (calorie per gram per degree Celsius). Numerically, water has $c = 1\text{ cal/g·°C}$ in CGS, making calculations simpler in this system.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>**Conversion between systems:** $1\text{ J/kg·K} = 0.000239\text{ cal/g·°C}$. Conversely, $1\text{ cal/g·°C} = 4184\text{ J/kg·K}$. The factor 4184 comes from $1\text{ cal} = 4.184\text{ J}$ and $1\text{ g} = 0.001\text{ kg}$.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>**Dimensional formula derivation:** $c = \frac{H}{m \cdot \Delta\theta}$. Heat $H$ has the same dimensions as energy: $[ML^2T^{-2}]$. Mass: $[M]$. Temperature: $[\Theta]$ (or $[K]$). Therefore: **$[c] = [M^0 L^2 T^{-2} \Theta^{-1}]$**.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>**Dimensionless mass:** Notice $[M^0]$ - mass cancels out. This reflects that specific heat is an INTENSIVE property. Two objects of the same material but different masses have the same $c$.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>**Alternative form using force:** Since work = force × distance, $[H] = [MLT^{-2}] \cdot [L] = [ML^2T^{-2}]$. So $[c] = [ML^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}] / ([M][\Theta]) = [L^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}]$.</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>**Common values in both systems:** Water: $1\text{ cal/g·°C} = 4186\text{ J/kg·K}$. Ice: $0.5\text{ cal/g·°C} = 2100\text{ J/kg·K}$. Copper: $0.093\text{ cal/g·°C} = 390\text{ J/kg·K}$. Aluminum: $0.215\text{ cal/g·°C} = 900\text{ J/kg·K}$.</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>**Dimensional check example:** For $H = mc\Delta\theta$: $[H] = [M] \cdot [L^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}] \cdot [\Theta] = [ML^2T^{-2}]$. This matches the dimension of energy. ✓</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>**Heat capacity dimensional formula:** $C = mc$, so $[C] = [M] \cdot [L^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}] = [ML^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}]$. Unlike specific heat, heat capacity DOES depend on mass (it's extensive).</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>**Molar heat capacity dimensions:** $C_m = c \times M$ (molar mass), so $[C_m] = [L^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}] \cdot [M] = [ML^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}]$. Same dimensions as specific heat, but per mole instead of per kg.</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>**In terms of base SI units:** $[c] = [kg^0 \cdot m^2 \cdot s^{-2} \cdot K^{-1}] = [m^2 \cdot s^{-2} \cdot K^{-1}]$. This is the simplest form - just length-squared per time-squared per kelvin.</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>**Why $\Theta$ appears in the denominator:** Specific heat is defined PER UNIT TEMPERATURE CHANGE. If $\Delta\theta$ doubles, $H$ doubles. The $\Theta^{-1}$ dimension captures this inverse proportionality.</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>**Practical importance of correct units:** In $H = mc\Delta\theta$, if $m$ is in kg and $c$ in J/kg·K, then $H$ is in joules. If $m$ is in g and $c$ in cal/g·°C, then $H$ is in calories. Mixing systems (e.g., kg with cal/g·°C) gives WRONG answers.</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** Be able to derive $[c] = [M^0L^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}]$, convert between J/kg·K and cal/g·°C, and use the correct units consistently in numerical problems.</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>**Dimensional homogeneity test:** Any correct formula for heat must have $[H] = [ML^2T^{-2}]$ on both sides. If a proposed formula gives different dimensions, it is immediately wrong - no calculation needed.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Key Point 2: Essential theorem or principle related to Specific Heat Capacity</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 3: Important condition or constraint for Specific Heat Capacity</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 4: Common applications of Specific Heat Capacity</td></tr><tr><td>Key Point 5: Connection to other topics in Science</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Carefully distinguish between similar-looking concepts in specific heat capacity.</td></tr><tr><td>Pay attention to the conditions under which formulas and theorems apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice with multiple examples to solidify your understanding.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Specific heat capacity is the same for all states of a substance. ✅ Ice ($c=2100$), liquid water ($c=4186$), and steam ($c=2010$) all have different specific heats. The value changes at phase transitions.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Specific heat depends on the shape/size of the object. ✅ Specific heat is an INTENSIVE property - it depends only on the material, not on the object's shape, size, or mass. A gold ring and a gold bar have the same $c$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ $c_p$ and $c_v$ are the same for all substances. ✅ For solids and liquids, $c_p \approx c_v$ because they barely expand. For gases, $c_p > c_v$ significantly because gases expand and do work. The difference is $c_p - c_v = R/M$ (per kg) or $R$ (per mole).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A substance with high specific heat is always "hotter." ✅ High $c$ means the substance RESISTS temperature change - it takes a lot of heat to raise its temperature. Water at 20°C has high $c$ but isn't "hot" - it just requires a lot of energy to change its temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The specific heat of water is 1 cal/g·°C everywhere. ✅ The calorie is DEFINED using water's specific heat, but that specific heat varies slightly with temperature. At 15°C, $c = 4.1855\text{ J/g·K}$ (giving 1 cal = 4.1855 J). The modern exact value is $1\text{ cal} = 4.184\text{ J}$ (thermochemical calorie).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Gases have only one specific heat capacity. ✅ Gases have TWO: $c_p$ (constant pressure) and $c_v$ (constant volume). $c_p - c_v = R$ per mole, or $c_p - c_v = R/M$ per unit mass. For air, $c_p = 1005$ and $c_v = 718\text{ J/kg·K}$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Metals always have low specific heat. ✅ While most metals do have low $c$ (because they're dense and atoms are heavy), beryllium ($c = 1825$) and lithium ($c = 3580$) are exceptions - they have low atomic mass, so per kg they have many atoms and high heat capacity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ $c$ has dimensions of $[ML^2T^{-2}]$. ✅ $c = H/(m\Delta\theta)$, so $[c] = [ML^2T^{-2}] / ([M][\Theta]) = [L^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}]$. Mass dimension CANCELS. The $[M^0]$ is the key difference from heat capacity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ J/kg·K and J/kg·°C are different units. ✅ Since these units involve a TEMPERATURE INTERVAL (Δθ), and 1 K = 1°C in size, J/kg·K and J/kg·°C are EXACTLY the same. Only absolute temperatures differ.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The dimensional formula depends on the system of units. ✅ Dimensional formulas are system-independent. Whether you use SI or CGS, $[c] = [L^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}]$. Only the numerical VALUES change, not the dimensions.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat capacity has the same dimensions as specific heat. ✅ Heat capacity $C = mc$ has $[C] = [ML^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}]$ - it includes the mass dimension $[M]$ because it depends on the amount of substance.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ $c$ can have negative dimensions. ✅ Specific heat is always positive for normal materials. Negative $c$ would mean adding heat lowers temperature, which violates thermodynamics (except in exotic gravitational systems like stars).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ $1\text{ cal/g·°C} = 1\text{ J/kg·K}$. ✅ They differ by a factor of 4184. The calorie is about 4184 times larger than the joule, and the gram is 1/1000 of a kilogram, so the ratio is $4184 \times 1000 / 1000 = 4184$.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Specific Heat Capacity is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics. Specific Heat Capacity (c) — Definition & Key Values covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Change of Phases and Latent Heat</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Heat is energy in transit:** Heat ($H$) is the thermal energy transferred between systems due to a temperature difference. It is NOT a property of a body - you cannot say "a body contains heat." A body contains internal energy; heat is the transfer of that energy.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Mass dependency (H ∝ m):** For a given substance and temperature change, the heat required is directly proportional to the mass: $H \propto m$. Doubling the mass doubles the heat needed. This is because more molecules means more total kinetic energy must change.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Temperature dependency (H ∝ Δθ):** For a given mass, heat is directly proportional to the temperature change: $H \propto \Delta\theta$, where $\Delta\theta = \theta_f - \theta_i$ (final − initial). Heating water from 20°C to 80°C requires 3× the heat of heating from 20°C to 40°C.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Combined relation:** Combining both: $H \propto m \cdot \Delta\theta$. Introducing the constant of proportionality - the specific heat capacity ($c$) - gives the fundamental equation: **$H = m \cdot c \cdot \Delta\theta$**</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Sign convention:** When heat is absorbed (temperature rises), $H > 0$ and $\Delta\theta > 0$. When heat is released (temperature falls), $H < 0$ and $\Delta\theta < 0$. The sign tells you the direction of energy flow.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Units of heat:** SI unit is the **joule (J)**. The CGS unit is the **calorie (cal)**. Conversion: $1\text{ cal} = 4.184\text{ J}$ (thermochemical calorie). Another unit, the BTU (British Thermal Unit), is used in engineering: $1\text{ BTU} \approx 1055\text{ J}$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Heat vs. Temperature:** Temperature ($\theta$) is an intensive property - it doesn't depend on the amount of substance. Heat ($H$) is extensive - it depends on mass. A cup of water at 80°C has a higher temperature than a bathtub at 50°C, but the bathtub has more thermal energy (and more heat to transfer if cooled).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Heat vs. Internal Energy:** Internal energy ($U$) is the total energy stored in a system's molecules. Heat ($H$) is the energy TRANSFERRED. When you heat a body, its internal energy increases: $\Delta U = H$ (at constant volume, no work done).</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Real-world analogy:** Think of water flowing from a high tank to a low tank. Temperature is like water level (height), heat is like the amount of water that flows. Two tanks at the same height have the same temperature, but the larger tank has more water (more heat capacity).</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Calorimetry context:** In all calorimetry problems, the equation $H = mc\Delta\theta$ is applied to EACH substance separately. Hot body: $H_{lost} = m_h c_h (\theta_h - \theta_{eq})$. Cold body: $H_{gained} = m_c c_c (\theta_{eq} - \theta_c)$. Set $H_{lost} = H_{gained}$.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Common values of c (for reference):** Water: $c = 4200\text{ J/kg·K}$. Ice: $c = 2100\text{ J/kg·K}$. Copper: $c = 390\text{ J/kg·K}$. Aluminum: $c = 900\text{ J/kg·K}$. Iron: $c = 450\text{ J/kg·K}$. Lead: $c = 130\text{ J/kg·K}$. Higher $c$ means more heat needed per degree.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Why water is special:** Water has the highest specific heat among common liquids ($4200\text{ J/kg·K}$). This means it absorbs huge amounts of heat with minimal temperature change - why it's used as a coolant in car radiators, power plants, and why coastal climates are moderate.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Quick estimation rule:** For a 1 kg mass of water, raising temperature by 1°C requires 4200 J. So heating 2 kg by 50°C requires $H = 2 \times 4200 \times 50 = 420{,}000\text{ J} = 420\text{ kJ}$. This is roughly the energy in 100 g of sugar.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Dimensional check:** $[H] = [M][L]^2[T]^{-2}$ (energy). $[mc\Delta\theta] = [M] \cdot [L]^2[T]^{-2}[\Theta]^{-1} \cdot [\Theta] = [M][L]^2[T]^{-2}$. ✓ Dimensions match.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Power and heat rate:** If heat is supplied at a constant rate (power $P$), then $H = Pt$. So $Pt = mc\Delta\theta$, which gives $\Delta\theta/t = P/(mc)$. A 1500 W kettle heats 1 kg of water at $\Delta\theta/t = 1500/(1 \times 4200) = 0.357\text{ °C/s}$.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Change of Phases and Latent Heat:** This topic covers the fundamental concepts of change of phases and latent heat in the physics curriculum.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Understanding change of phases and latent heat is essential for solving problems and building higher-level mathematical skills.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Key definitions and theorems related to change of phases and latent heat should be memorized and practiced regularly.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat and temperature are the same thing. ✅ Temperature is the average molecular kinetic energy (intensive). Heat is the total energy transferred (extensive). A spark at 2000°C has tiny heat content; a bathtub at 40°C has huge heat content.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A body "contains" heat. ✅ A body contains internal energy (kinetic + potential of its molecules). Heat is the TRANSFER of energy due to temperature difference. Once energy is in the body, it's no longer "heat." Think of it like "payment" - once received, it's no longer a payment; it's just money in your account.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All materials heat up at the same rate with the same power. ✅ Different materials have different specific heats. 1 kW heats 1 kg of aluminum ($c=900$) much faster than 1 kg of water ($c=4200$). The aluminum's temperature rises ~4.7× faster.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The formula H = mcΔθ works for phase changes too. ✅ This formula ONLY works when temperature changes WITHOUT a phase change. During melting (ice → water at 0°C) or boiling (water → steam at 100°C), temperature stays constant - you need $H = mL$ instead.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Specific heat capacity depends on the amount of substance. ✅ Specific heat capacity is an INTENSIVE property - it depends only on the MATERIAL, not the mass. 1 kg of copper has $c = 390\text{ J/kg·K}$, same as 1 g of copper.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat always flows from hot to cold, so a cold object can never lose heat to a hot one. ✅ Heat naturally flows from hot to cold. However, a refrigerator CAN move heat from cold to hot - but only by doing external work (compressor). This doesn't violate the second law.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ If you add heat, temperature always rises. ✅ During a phase change (melting, boiling, sublimation), added heat goes into breaking intermolecular bonds (increasing potential energy), NOT increasing kinetic energy. Temperature stays constant during the transition.</td></tr><tr><td>Carefully distinguish between similar-looking concepts in change of phases and latent heat.</td></tr><tr><td>Pay attention to the conditions under which formulas and theorems apply.</td></tr><tr><td>Practice with multiple examples to solidify your understanding.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Change of Phases and Latent Heat is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Heat Capacity (Thermal Capacity, C')</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Definition:** Heat capacity (also called thermal capacity), denoted $C'$ or simply $C$, is the amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of an **entire object** (not a unit mass) by 1 K (or 1°C).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Formula:** $C' = m \cdot c$, where $m$ is the mass of the object and $c$ is the specific heat capacity of the material. Alternatively, $C' = H/\Delta\theta$ - heat required per degree of temperature rise.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Heat exchanged using C':** $H = C' \cdot \Delta\theta$. This is simpler than $H = mc\Delta\theta$ when you already know the heat capacity of the object.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**SI unit:** $\text{J/K}$ (joule per kelvin) or $\text{J/°C}$. Since a kelvin and a degree Celsius have the same size, $\text{J/K} = \text{J/°C}$ numerically.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**CGS unit:** $\text{cal/°C}$ (calorie per degree Celsius). Water's heat capacity is $1\text{ cal/°C}$ per gram, or $4.186\text{ J/°C}$ per gram.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Key distinction - C' vs c:** $c$ (specific heat) is an INTENSIVE property - it depends only on the MATERIAL (e.g., copper always has $c = 390\text{ J/kg·K}$). $C'$ (heat capacity) is an EXTENSIVE property - it depends on both the material AND the mass of the object. A 2 kg copper block has $C' = 2 \times 390 = 780\text{ J/K}$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Dimensional formula:** $[C'] = [M] \cdot [L^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}] = [ML^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}]$. Unlike $c$, the mass dimension $[M]$ does NOT cancel.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Water equivalent connection:** Heat capacity is directly related to water equivalent: $C' = W \cdot c_w$, where $W$ is the water equivalent mass and $c_w$ is water's specific heat. So $C' = W \times 4186\text{ J/K}$.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Calorimeter heat capacity:** A calorimeter has its own heat capacity $C'_{cal} = m_{cal} \cdot c_{cal}$. In calorimetry problems, this must be included: $H_{gained} = (m_{liquid}c_{liquid} + C'_{cal})\Delta\theta_{liquid}$.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Practical examples:** A 500 g aluminum pot ($c = 900$): $C' = 0.5 \times 900 = 450\text{ J/K}$. A 2 kg iron pan ($c = 450$): $C' = 2 \times 450 = 900\text{ J/K}$. The iron pan needs more heat for the same temperature rise - despite iron's lower $c$, its larger mass wins.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Why it matters in experiments:** When measuring specific heat using the method of mixtures, you must account for the heat capacity of the container. Neglecting $C'_{cal}$ is a common source of error.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Thermal mass:** In building physics</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>thermal mass" is the aggregate heat capacity of a building's materials (walls, floors, furniture). High thermal mass means the building heats and cools slowly - beneficial for passive solar design.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Comparison table:** Substance | Mass | c (J/kg·K) | C' (J/K)
Water (1 L) | 1 kg | 4200 | 4200
Aluminum pot (0.5 kg) | 0.5 kg | 900 | 450
Iron pan (2 kg) | 2 kg | 450 | 900
Lead weight (1 kg) | 1 kg | 130 | 130</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**NEB exam tip:** Distinguish clearly between $c$ (specific heat, per kg) and $C'$ (heat capacity, for the whole object). Many exam questions give $C'$ directly and ask you to find $c$, or vice versa. Remember: $C' = mc$ and $c = C'/m$.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Relationship to Newton's law of cooling:** In Newton's law, the cooling constant $K$ is often written as $K = hA/C'$, where $h$ is the surface heat transfer coefficient and $A$ is the surface area. A larger $C'$ means slower cooling.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Connection of Heat Capacity (Thermal Capacity, C′) to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat capacity and specific heat are the same thing. ✅ Heat capacity ($C'$) depends on the object's mass; specific heat ($c$) is a property of the material alone. $C' = mc$ - they are related but not identical.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A larger object always has a larger heat capacity. ✅ For the SAME material, yes. But a small lead weight ($c=130$) may have a smaller $C'$ than a large aluminum block ($c=900$) even if the lead is more massive.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat capacity has no units. ✅ Heat capacity has units: J/K (SI) or cal/°C (CGS). Specific heat has units J/kg·K or cal/g·°C. The units are different by a factor of mass.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat capacity is the same for all objects of the same material. ✅ Heat capacity depends on BOTH material and mass. Two copper objects have the same $c$ but different $C'$ if their masses differ.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The dimensional formula of heat capacity is $[M^0L^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}]$. ✅ Heat capacity includes mass: $[C'] = [ML^2T^{-2}\Theta^{-1}]$. The $[M]$ does NOT cancel because $C'$ depends on the amount of substance.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ You can use $C'$ in place of $c$ in all formulas. ✅ In $H = mc\Delta\theta$, replace $mc$ with $C'$: $H = C'\Delta\theta$. But if you need $c$ itself (e.g., to identify the material), you must divide: $c = C'/m$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Water equivalent has units of J/K. ✅ Water equivalent ($W$) has units of MASS (kg or g). Heat capacity has units of J/K. They are related by $C' = W \cdot c_w$, where $c_w = 4186\text{ J/kg·K}$.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Heat Capacity (Thermal Capacity, C′) covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Specific latent heat of fusion and vaporization</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Specific latent heat of fusion and vaporization — Quantity of Heat, Physics (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: specific latent heat of fusion, vaporization · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** Heat Q = mcΔT; specific heat c, thermal capacity C = mc, heat capacity per mole = C. molar.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** Latent heat L: Q = mL (change of state at constant T). Calorimetry: heat lost = heat gained.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** Newton's law of cooling: rate of heat loss ∝ (T − T₀); dθ/dt = −k(θ − θ₀).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Vaporization.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Specific latent heat of fusion</td></tr><tr><td>• Vaporization</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Specific latent heat of fusion" as one blob → ✅ split into: specific latent heat of fusion, vaporization.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ heat q = mcδt; specific heat c, thermal capacity c = mc, heat capacity per mole = c. molar..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Specific latent heat of fusion and vaporization (Quantity of Heat, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Specific latent heat of fusion, vaporization. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Triple Point</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The triple point is the unique pressure and temperature at which solid, liquid and vapour of a pure substance coexist in equilibrium. It is a point on the phase diagram, not the same as a normal-pressure melting or boiling point.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Water’s triple point is approximately 273.16 K (0.01°C) and 611.7 Pa. At ordinary atmospheric pressure, ice and liquid water coexist near 0°C at a different pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>While all three phases coexist, supplied energy can change their relative amounts while pressure and temperature remain fixed. Equal quantities of each phase are not required.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>On a pressure–temperature diagram, the solid–liquid, liquid–vapour and solid–vapour boundary curves meet at the triple point. It provides a reproducible reference state for thermometry.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>A phase diagram shows why pressure must accompany temperature when describing a phase equilibrium; changing pressure can shift the phase boundaries.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Three phases coexist at one specific pair of pressure and temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure matters; temperature alone does not define the triple point.</td></tr><tr><td>Phase amounts need not be equal.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Do not quote 0°C and 1 atm as the triple-point conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>The definition applies to one pure substance in equilibrium.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The triple point is the unique P–T state where solid, liquid and vapour coexist. Water’s value is about 273.16 K and 611.7 Pa.</p><hr><h2>Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion and vaporiz…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion and vaporiz… — Quantity of Heat, Physics (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: measurement of specific latent heat of fusion, vaporization · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** Heat Q = mcΔT; specific heat c, thermal capacity C = mc, heat capacity per mole = C. molar.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** Latent heat L: Q = mL (change of state at constant T). Calorimetry: heat lost = heat gained.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** Newton's law of cooling: rate of heat loss ∝ (T − T₀); dθ/dt = −k(θ − θ₀).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Vaporization.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion</td></tr><tr><td>• Vaporization</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion" as one blob → ✅ split into: measurement of specific latent heat of fusion, vaporization.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ no diagram → ✅ add one labelled figure / structured list.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ heat q = mcδt; specific heat c, thermal capacity c = mc, heat capacity per mole = c. molar..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion and vaporiz… (Quantity of Heat, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion, vaporization. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Principle of Calorimetry (Method of Mixtures)</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Statement:** When two or more bodies at different temperatures are brought into thermal contact in an isolated system, heat flows from hotter to colder bodies until they reach a common final temperature (thermal equilibrium).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Core principle:** In the absence of heat loss to the surroundings: **Heat lost by hot bodies = Heat gained by cold bodies**.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Mathematical form:** For two bodies: $m_1 c_1 (\theta_1 - \theta_{eq}) = m_2 c_2 (\theta_{eq} - \theta_2)$, where $\theta_1 > \theta_{eq} > \theta_2$.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Including the calorimeter:** In real experiments, the calorimeter vessel also absorbs/releases heat. The full equation is: $m_h c_h (\theta_h - \theta_{eq}) = (m_c c_c + C'_{cal})(\theta_{eq} - \theta_c)$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Water equivalent form:** If the calorimeter's water equivalent $W$ is known: $m_h c_h (\theta_h - \theta_{eq}) = (m_c c_c + W c_w)(\theta_{eq} - \theta_c)$.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Derivation from energy conservation:** The total energy of the isolated system is constant: $\Delta U_{total} = 0$. Since $\Delta U = mc\Delta\theta$ (no phase change), $\sum mc\theta_{final} - \sum mc\theta_{initial} = 0$, which rearranges to the method of mixtures equation.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Finding an unknown specific heat:** If you know $m_h$, $c_h$, $\theta_h$, $m_c$, $\theta_c$, and measure $\theta_{eq}$, you can solve for the unknown $c_c$: $c_c = \frac{m_h c_h (\theta_h - \theta_{eq})}{m_c(\theta_{eq} - \theta_c)}$.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Finding an unknown mass:** Similarly, if you know the specific heats but not the mass of one body, you can find the mass from the equilibrium temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Finding the equilibrium temperature:** $\theta_{eq} = \frac{m_1 c_1 \theta_1 + m_2 c_2 \theta_2}{m_1 c_1 + m_2 c_2}$. This is a weighted average of the initial temperatures, weighted by heat capacities.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Calorimeter setup:** A typical calorimeter is a copper vessel (good conductor, low $c$) nested inside an insulating jacket (wood, cork, or air gap) to minimize heat loss. A stirrer ensures uniform temperature, and a thermometer measures the temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Assumptions in the method:** (1) No heat is lost to the surroundings (adiabatic). (2) Heat transfer is complete - all bodies reach the same temperature. (3) No phase change occurs. (4) The calorimeter is in thermal equilibrium with its contents at each reading.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Error sources:** Heat loss to surroundings (always occurs to some degree) makes $\theta_{eq}$ slightly lower than ideal, leading to overestimation of $c$ for the cold body. Radiation, convection, and conduction through supports all contribute.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Newton's cooling correction:** To minimize heat-loss error, the experiment is often arranged so that the initial temperature excess above room temperature equals the final temperature deficit below room temperature. The gains and losses then approximately cancel.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** Method of mixtures problems are extremely common. You MUST include the calorimeter's heat capacity (or water equivalent) in your equation. Forgetting it is the most common error.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Quick check:** After solving for $\theta_{eq}$, verify that $\theta_h > \theta_{eq} > \theta_c$. If this is violated, you made an error in sign conventions.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Connection of Principle of Calorimetry (Method of Mixtures) to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat lost + heat gained = 0, so $m_1c_1\Delta\theta_1 + m_2c_2\Delta\theta_2 = 0$ means both Δθ are positive. ✅ Use signed convention: $\Delta\theta_1 = \theta_{eq} - \theta_h < 0$ (negative, heat lost) and $\Delta\theta_2 = \theta_{eq} - \theta_c > 0$ (positive, heat gained). The sum is zero.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ You can ignore the calorimeter's heat capacity. ✅ The calorimeter is in thermal contact with both substances and reaches the same $\theta_{eq}$. Its heat capacity $C'_{cal}$ can be significant - especially for thick or non-copper calorimeters.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The equilibrium temperature is the arithmetic mean of the two initial temperatures. ✅ Only true if $m_1c_1 = m_2c_2$ (equal heat capacities). Generally: $\theta_{eq} = \frac{m_1c_1\theta_1 + m_2c_2\theta_2}{m_1c_1 + m_2c_2}$ - a WEIGHTED average.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat lost by hot body equals heat gained by cold body even if heat escapes to surroundings. ✅ This equality holds ONLY for an isolated (adiabatic) system. In practice, some heat is always lost, making the equation an approximation.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ You need to know the specific heat of both substances to find the equilibrium temperature. ✅ You need $m$ and $c$ for both, OR you can work with heat capacities directly if they're given. If one $c$ is unknown, you can find it using a known reference substance.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The method of mixtures works for gases. ✅ The method is designed for solids and liquids. Gases expand and do work, so $c_p 
eq c_v$ and the analysis is more complex. The method works best for incompressible substances.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Phase changes can be included in the method of mixtures using the same formula. ✅ Phase changes require $H = mL$ instead of $H = mc\Delta\theta$. If a phase change occurs, you must include the latent heat term separately.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Principle of Calorimetry (Method of Mixtures) covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Newton's Law of Cooling - Statement</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Statement:** The rate of loss of heat of a body is directly proportional to the difference between the body's temperature ($\theta$) and the surrounding temperature ($\theta_0$), provided the temperature difference is small (typically $< 30°\text{C}$).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Mathematical form:** $\frac{dH}{dt} = -k(\theta - \theta_0)$, where $k > 0$ is a constant depending on the body's properties and surface area. The negative sign indicates heat is LOST (temperature decreasing).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**In terms of temperature rate:** Since $dH = mc\,d\theta$, we get $\frac{d\theta}{dt} = -\frac{k}{mc}(\theta - \theta_0) = -K'(\theta - \theta_0)$, where $K' = \frac{k}{mc}$ is the cooling constant.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**The constant $k$:** $k$ depends on (i) the nature of the body - material, surface texture, emissivity; (ii) the surface area exposed to surroundings; (iii) the nature of the surrounding medium (air, water). A blackened surface cools faster than a polished one.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Applicability condition:** Newton's law is an APPROXIMATION valid only when $(\theta - \theta_0)$ is small. For large temperature differences, Stefan-Boltzmann radiation law ($\frac{dH}{dt} \propto \theta^4 - \theta_0^4$) must be used.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Physical basis:** At small temperature differences, the radiation heat transfer ($\theta^4 - \theta_0^4 \approx 4\theta_0^3(\theta - \theta_0)$) is approximately linear in $(\theta - \theta_0)$. Convection also contributes a linear term.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Cooling curve:** Plotting $\theta$ vs. $t$ gives an exponential decay curve. The slope $\frac{d\theta}{dt}$ is steepest at the beginning (large $\theta - \theta_0$) and gradually approaches zero as $\theta \to \theta_0$.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Time constant:** The characteristic cooling time is $\tau = \frac{1}{K'} = \frac{mc}{k}$. After time $\tau$, the temperature difference drops to $1/e \approx 37\%$ of its initial value. A larger mass or higher $c$ means slower cooling.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Surface area effect:** A sphere and a cube of the same material and mass cool at different rates because they have different surface areas. More surface area → larger $k$ → faster cooling. This is why thermos flasks have small openings.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Surface texture effect:** A dull, blackened surface has high emissivity and radiates/absorbs heat efficiently. A polished, shiny surface has low emissivity and cools slowly. This is why survival blankets are shiny.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Everyday examples:** A cup of hot coffee cools faster on a windy day (convection increases $k$). A thermos flask slows cooling by reducing conduction and convection (vacuum gap). A radiator in a car has fins to maximize surface area.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Experimental verification:** Heat a liquid (e.g., water or oil) in a copper calorimeter. Record temperature at regular time intervals. Plot $\theta$ vs. $t$ - you should get a smooth exponential decay curve approaching $\theta_0$.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Connection to RC circuits:** The mathematical form $\frac{d\theta}{dt} = -K'(\theta - \theta_0)$ is identical to the charging/discharging of a capacitor: $\frac{dV}{dt} = -\frac{1}{RC}(V - V_0)$. Temperature is analogous to voltage; thermal resistance to electrical resistance.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** State Newton's law, write the differential equation, identify what $k$ depends on, explain the small-difference condition, and distinguish Newton's law from the Stefan-Boltzmann law.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Common exam trap:** The law applies to the RATE of heat loss, not the total heat lost. $\frac{dH}{dt} \propto (\theta - \theta_0)$, not $H \propto (\theta - \theta_0)$.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Differential equation:** $\frac{d\theta}{dt} = -K'(\theta - \theta_0)$, where $K' = k/(mc)$ is the cooling constant.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>**Separation of variables:** $\frac{d\theta}{\theta - \theta_0} = -K'\,dt$. Integrate both sides: $\int\frac{d\theta}{\theta - \theta_0} = -K'\int dt$.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>**Integration result:** $\ln(\theta - \theta_0) = -K't + C$, where $C$ is the integration constant.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>**Applying initial condition:** At $t = 0$, $\theta = \theta_i$. So $C = \ln(\theta_i - \theta_0)$. Therefore: $\ln(\theta - \theta_0) = -K't + \ln(\theta_i - \theta_0)$.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>**Exponential solution:** $\boxed{\theta(t) - \theta_0 = (\theta_i - \theta_0)\,e^{-K't}}$. This is the key result — temperature decays exponentially toward the surrounding temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>**Temperature excess:** Let $\Delta\theta(t) = \theta(t) - \theta_0$ be the temperature excess. Then $\Delta\theta(t) = \Delta\theta_i \cdot e^{-K't}$. The excess decays exponentially.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>**Half-life of cooling:** The time for the temperature excess to halve is $t_{1/2} = \frac{\ln 2}{K'} \approx \frac{0.693}{K'}$. After each half-life, the excess is halved again.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>**Time constant:** $\tau = \frac{1}{K'} = \frac{mc}{k}$. After time $\tau$, the excess is $1/e \approx 36.8\%$ of its initial value. This is the characteristic timescale of cooling.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>**Graph of θ vs. t:** The curve starts at $\theta_i$, decreases rapidly at first (steep slope = large $d\theta/dt$), and gradually flattens as it approaches $\theta_0$ asymptotically. The curve NEVER reaches $\theta_0$ in finite time.</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>**Graph of dθ/dt vs. (θ - θ₀):** This is a straight line through the origin with slope $-K'$. It shows the linear relationship at the heart of Newton's law.</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>**Graph of ln(θ - θ₀) vs. t:** This is a straight line with slope $-K'$ and intercept $\ln(\theta_i - \theta_0)$. This linear form is useful for experimental verification — plot the data and check for linearity.</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>**Asymptotic behavior:** As $t \to \infty$, $e^{-K't} \to 0$, so $\theta \to \theta_0$. The body approaches thermal equilibrium with its surroundings but never quite reaches it in finite time.</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>**Reversibility for heating:** If $\theta_i < \theta_0$ (body starts colder than surroundings), the same equation applies: $\theta(t) - \theta_0 = (\theta_i - \theta_0)e^{-K't}$. Since $(\theta_i - \theta_0)$ is negative, $\theta(t)$ increases toward $\theta_0$.</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>**Numerical example:** A body at 80°C in a 20°C room with $K' = 0.05\text{ min}^{-1}$. After 10 min: $\theta = 20 + 60 \times e^{-0.5} = 20 + 60 \times 0.607 = 56.4°\text{C}$. After 20 min: $\theta = 20 + 60 \times e^{-1} = 20 + 22.1 = 42.1°\text{C}$. After 40 min: $\theta = 20 + 60 \times e^{-2} = 20 + 8.1 = 28.1°\text{C}$.</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** Derive the exponential solution from the differential equation. Sketch the cooling curve. Identify the half-life and time constant. Use the formula to calculate temperatures at given times.</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>**Identical conditions defined:** Two bodies are cooled under identical conditions when they have the same surface area, same surface texture/emissivity, same shape, and are placed in the same surroundings (same $\theta_0$, same air movement).</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>**Equal rate of heat loss:** Under identical conditions, the heat-loss constant $k$ is the same for both bodies. Therefore, at the same temperature excess $(\theta - \theta_0)$: $\frac{dH_1}{dt} = \frac{dH_2}{dt} = k(\theta - \theta_0)$.</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>**Different rate of temperature fall:** Since $\frac{dH}{dt} = mc\frac{d\theta}{dt}$, equal heat loss rates give: $m_1 c_1 \frac{d\theta_1}{dt} = m_2 c_2 \frac{d\theta_2}{dt}$. Therefore: $\boxed{\frac{(d\theta/dt)_1}{(d\theta/dt)_2} = \frac{m_2 c_2}{m_1 c_1}}$.</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>**Key insight:** Equal heat loss does NOT mean equal temperature change. The body with the SMALLER heat capacity ($mc$) experiences a LARGER temperature change for the same heat loss.</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>**Practical application — comparing specific heats:** If two liquids are cooled under identical conditions and you measure their rates of temperature fall at the same temperature, you can find the ratio of their specific heats: $\frac{c_1}{c_2} = \frac{(d\theta/dt)_2}{(d\theta/dt)_1} \times \frac{m_2}{m_1}$.</td></tr><tr><td>36</td><td>**Equal temperature fall:** If two bodies show the SAME rate of temperature fall under identical conditions, their heat capacities must be equal: $m_1 c_1 = m_2 c_2$. This is the basis for comparing substances.</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>**Method of cooling experiment:** (1) Take equal masses of two liquids (water and an unknown liquid) in identical calorimeters. (2) Heat both to the same initial temperature. (3) Record the time for each to cool through the same temperature interval. (4) Use $\frac{(m_1 c_1 + W)c_1}{t_1} = \frac{(m_2 c_2 + W)c_2}{t_2}$ to find the unknown $c_2$.</td></tr><tr><td>38</td><td>**Water as reference:** Since water's specific heat is known ($c_w = 4186\text{ J/kg·K}$), it serves as the reference substance. If liquid X takes time $t_x$ and water takes time $t_w$ to cool through the same interval: $\frac{c_x}{c_w} = \frac{t_w}{t_x}$ (ignoring calorimeter).</td></tr><tr><td>39</td><td>**Calorimeter correction:** Always include the water equivalent $W$ of the calorimeter: $\frac{(m_x c_x + W)c_w}{t_x} = \frac{(m_w c_w + W)c_w}{t_w}$. If $W$ is neglected, the result has systematic error.</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>**Why identical conditions matter:** If one calorimeter has a larger opening or a different surface finish, $k$ will differ, and the comparison is invalid. The experiment must control for all geometric and surface factors.</td></tr><tr><td>41</td><td>**Surface area effect:** A wider calorimeter cools faster (larger $A$ → larger $k$). If the two calorimeters have different shapes, even with the same material, the cooling rates won't be comparable.</td></tr><tr><td>42</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** Understand that identical conditions → equal $k$. Use $\frac{m_1 c_1}{t_1} = \frac{m_2 c_2}{t_2}$ (with calorimeter correction) to compare specific heats. Know why equal heat loss doesn't mean equal temperature change.</td></tr><tr><td>43</td><td>**Real-world example:** A copper cup and an aluminum cup of the same size, filled with equal masses of hot water, will cool at the same RATE OF HEAT LOSS (same $k$, same excess temperature) but the aluminum cup's contents may cool faster in temperature if the cup itself absorbs more heat.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Connection of Newton's Law of Cooling — Statement to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Newton's law says temperature is proportional to time. ✅ It says the RATE of temperature change is proportional to the temperature difference: $\frac{d\theta}{dt} \propto (\theta - \theta_0)$. The solution is exponential, not linear.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Newton's law applies to all temperature differences. ✅ It is only accurate when $\theta - \theta_0 < 30°\text{C}$ approximately. For large differences, use Stefan-Boltzmann: rate $\propto \theta^4 - \theta_0^4$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The constant $k$ is the same for all bodies. ✅ $k$ depends on the body's surface area, texture, and the surrounding medium. Two identical bodies in different environments have different $k$ values.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A black body cools slower than a shiny body. ✅ A black/dull body has HIGHER emissivity and cools FASTER. This is why radiators are painted black and survival blankets are shiny (to retain heat).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Newton's law and Stefan-Boltzmann law give the same result always. ✅ They agree when $(\theta - \theta_0)$ is small. For large differences, Stefan-Boltzmann gives a much faster cooling rate (proportional to $T^4$).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The surrounding temperature must be zero for Newton's law. ✅ $\theta_0$ is the temperature of the surroundings (e.g., room temperature ≈ 25°C). The law uses the DIFFERENCE $(\theta - \theta_0)$, not absolute temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Newton's law applies to heating as well as cooling. ✅ Yes! If $\theta < \theta_0$, the body GAINS heat and the rate is positive: $\frac{d\theta}{dt} = +K'(\theta_0 - \theta)$. The same law describes both heating and cooling.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The body reaches room temperature in finite time. ✅ The exponential function $e^{-K't}$ approaches zero asymptotically but never reaches zero. The body gets arbitrarily close to $\theta_0$ but never exactly reaches it.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The cooling curve is a straight line. ✅ The cooling curve $\theta(t)$ is an exponential decay, not a straight line. The slope $d\theta/dt$ decreases with time. Only the plot of $\ln(\theta - \theta_0)$ vs. $t$ is a straight line.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The half-life depends on the initial temperature. ✅ The half-life $t_{1/2} = \ln 2/K'$ depends ONLY on the cooling constant, not on $\theta_i$. Each half-life reduces the excess by half, regardless of the starting excess.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A larger $K'$ means slower cooling. ✅ A larger $K'$ means FASTER cooling. $K' = k/(mc)$ — larger $k$ (better heat transfer) or smaller $mc$ (less thermal inertia) gives faster cooling.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The exponential solution only works for cooling, not heating. ✅ The same equation $\theta(t) - \theta_0 = (\theta_i - \theta_0)e^{-K't}$ works for both. For heating, $(\theta_i - \theta_0)$ is negative, so $\theta(t)$ increases toward $\theta_0$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ After one time constant, the body has cooled halfway. ✅ After one time constant $\tau$, the excess is $1/e \approx 36.8\%$ of its initial value, not 50%. The half-life is $t_{1/2} = \tau \ln 2 \approx 0.693\tau$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ You can use the approximate method (average rate) for large temperature differences. ✅ The approximate method $\frac{\Delta\theta}{\Delta t} = K'(\theta_{avg} - \theta_0)$ is only accurate for small temperature intervals. For large intervals, use the exact exponential formula.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Identical cooling conditions mean the bodies cool at the same rate of temperature fall. ✅ Identical conditions mean equal heat-loss constant $k$. The temperature fall rates can differ if the heat capacities ($mc$) differ.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ If two bodies cool at the same temperature rate, they must be the same material. ✅ They must have the same heat capacity ($mc$), but different combinations are possible: a small mass of high-$c$ material can equal a large mass of low-$c$ material.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The calorimeter's heat capacity can be ignored in the method of cooling. ✅ The calorimeter absorbs/releases the same heat as the liquid. For accurate results, include $W$ (water equivalent) in the equation.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Two bodies with the same mass always cool at the same rate under identical conditions. ✅ Same mass is not enough — they must also have the same specific heat (same $mc$). Different materials with the same mass cool at different rates.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The method of cooling requires equal masses of the two liquids. ✅ It requires equal volumes in identical calorimeters. The masses should be measured and used in the calculation. Equal volumes don't guarantee equal masses if densities differ.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Newton's law of cooling applies to the method of cooling experiment without restriction. ✅ The experiment works best when the temperature excess is small (< 30°C). For large temperature ranges, the law is approximate and results may be inaccurate.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A larger calorimeter (more surface area) always cools faster. ✅ For the SAME liquid and same initial temperature, a larger surface area increases $k$, leading to faster cooling. But if the larger calorimeter also holds more liquid, the effect on temperature rate depends on the ratio $k/(mc)$.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Newton's Law of Cooling — Statement covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Triple point</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Definition.** The triple point of a substance is the single temperature and pressure at which its solid, liquid and vapour phases coexist in stable equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Water's triple point.** For water it occurs at 273.16 K (0.01 °C) and 611.73 Pa (about 4.58 mmHg) — the fixed reference point of the Kelvin temperature scale.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Phase diagram.** On a pressure–temperature diagram the triple point is where the melting, boiling and sublimation curves meet; below its pressure a solid sublimes instead of melting.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Per-substance value.** Each pure substance has its own triple point (for CO₂ it is −56.6 °C at 5.1 atm), so the value identifies the substance's phase behaviour.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Thermometric use.** Because it is reproducible and substance-specific, the triple point of water is used to calibrate precision thermometers.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Triple point</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Triple Point to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Triple point (Quantity of Heat, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Triple point. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these. Triple Point covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Finding an Unknown Specific Heat Capacity by Comparing Cooling Rates</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Definition:** Specific heat capacity ($c$) is the amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of **1 kg** (SI) or **1 g** (CGS) of a substance by **1 K** (or 1°C). It is an intrinsic property of the material.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Formula:** From $H = mc\Delta\theta$, solving for $c$: **$c = \frac{H}{m \cdot \Delta\theta}$**. This shows $c$ is the heat per unit mass per unit temperature change.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Water - the benchmark:** $c_{water} = 4186\text{ J/kg·K}$ (more precisely 4181.3 J/kg·K at 15°C, but 4200 J/kg·K is standard for exam work). This is unusually high compared to most substances, making water an excellent thermal buffer.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Ice vs. liquid water:** $c_{ice} \approx 2100\text{ J/kg·K}$ - about HALF of liquid water's specific heat. This means ice heats up twice as fast as liquid water for the same mass. During melting, the $c$ changes discontinuously.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Metals - low specific heats:** Copper: $c = 390\text{ J/kg·K}$, Aluminum: $c = 900\text{ J/kg·K}$, Iron: $c = 450\text{ J/kg·K}$, Lead: $c = 130\text{ J/kg·K}$, Gold: $c = 129\text{ J/kg·K}$. Low $c$ means metals respond quickly to heating - useful for cookware, bad for thermal storage.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Solids (Dulong-Petit Law):** For most solid elements at room temperature, $c \times M \approx 25\text{ J/mol·K}$ (molar heat capacity). So $c \approx 25/M$ where $M$ is molar mass in kg/mol. Heavy atoms → low $c$ per kg; light atoms → high $c$ per kg.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Liquids:** Water: 4186, Ethanol: 2440, Mercury: 140, Glycerin: 2430, Ammonia (liquid): 4700. Gases have two specific heats ($c_p$ and $c_v$); most liquids have only one since they don't expand significantly.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Gases - two specific heats:** For gases, $c_p > c_v$ always. At constant pressure, gas expands and does work, needing MORE heat for the same temperature rise. For air: $c_p = 1005\text{ J/kg·K}$, $c_v = 718\text{ J/kg·K}$. Ratio $\gamma = c_p/c_v \approx 1.4$.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Temperature dependence:** Specific heat capacity is NOT truly constant - it varies with temperature. For water, $c$ changes from 4217 J/kg·K at 0°C to 4181 J/kg·K at 15°C to 4219 J/kg·K at 100°C. For most school problems, treat $c$ as constant.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Molar heat capacity vs. specific heat:** Molar heat capacity $C_m = c \times M$ (where $M$ = molar mass). For water: $C_m = 4186 \times 0.018 = 75.3\text{ J/mol·K}$. This is useful for comparing substances on a per-molecule basis.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Why specific heat differs:** The specific heat depends on how energy is distributed among molecular degrees of freedom. Monatomic gases store energy only in translation (3 DOF). Diatomic gases also store in rotation (2 DOF). Polyatomic molecules add vibration. More ways to store energy → higher $c$.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Water's anomalous value:** Water's $c = 4186$ is about 5× that of copper and 10× that of lead. This is because water molecules form hydrogen bonds that must be partially broken before molecules can move faster (higher temperature). This "hidden" energy cost makes $c$ high.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Practical - mixing two substances:** When a hot substance (mass $m_1$, $c_1$, $T_1$) is mixed with a cold substance ($m_2$, $c_2$, $T_2$), the equilibrium temperature is: $T_{eq} = \frac{m_1 c_1 T_1 + m_2 c_2 T_2}{m_1 c_1 + m_2 c_2}$. This is a weighted average by heat capacity.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**NEB focus:** Know the formula $H = mc\Delta\theta$, the values of $c$ for water and common metals, the distinction between $c$ and heat capacity ($C = mc$), and unit conversion between J/kg·K and cal/g·°C ($1\text{ J/kg·K} = 0.000239\text{ cal/g·°C}$).</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Common misconception:** "Specific" means "per unit mass" - it does NOT mean "typical" or "usual." Specific heat capacity is a very specific property that varies enormously between materials. Gold ($c=129$) and water ($c=4186$) differ by a factor of 32.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Newton's law of cooling states $\frac{dT}{dt} = -k(T - T_0)$, where $k$ is a constant depending on surface area, nature of surface, and surrounding medium. For two bodies of different materials but **identical shape, size, and surface conditions** cooling in the **same surroundings**, the cooling constant $k$ is the same.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Rate of heat loss: $\frac{dQ}{dt} = -mc\frac{dT}{dt}$. Under identical cooling conditions (same $T$, same $T_0$, same $k$), the cooling rates are proportional to $mc$: $m_1c_1\left(\frac{dT}{dt}\right)_1 = m_2c_2\left(\frac{dT}{dt}\right)_2$.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>**This eliminates the need to measure heat energy in joules** — we only compare cooling rates (temperature drop per unit time). The unknown specific heat $c_2$ is found using a reference liquid of known $c_1$ (usually water, $c_w = 4200\text{ J/kg·K}$ or $1\text{ cal/g·°C}$).</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>**Formula:** $c_2 = \frac{m_1c_1}{m_2} \times \frac{(dT/dt)_1}{(dT/dt)_2} = \frac{m_1c_1}{m_2} \times \frac{t_2}{t_1}$, where $t_1$ and $t_2$ are times for the same temperature drop $\Delta T$.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>**Key assumptions:** (1) Same calorimeter/cup used for both liquids, (2) Same volume of liquid (same mass if densities differ, account for it), (3) Same initial temperature and surroundings, (4) Temperature range small enough for Newton's law to hold ($<30^\circ\text{C}$ difference), (5) No phase change occurs.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>In practice, cooling rate is measured as time for temperature to fall through a fixed range (e.g., $60^\circ\text{C} \to 50^\circ\text{C}$). Since $\Delta T$ is constant, $\frac{dT}{dt} \propto \frac{1}{t}$. So $c_2 = \frac{m_1c_1 t_2}{m_2 t_1}$.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>**Water as reference:** Water's specific heat is exceptionally well-known ($c_w = 4186\text{ J/kg·K}$ at $20^\circ\text{C}$), making it ideal. For oils or organic liquids, $c$ typically ranges $1500	ext{--}2500\text{ J/kg·K}$.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>**Solid samples:** For solids, the method uses a calorimeter with the solid immersed in a known mass of water. The solid's cooling curve is compared with water's. More commonly, **method of mixtures** is used for solids; method of cooling is primarily for liquids.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>**Dimensional check:** $[c] = \text{L}^2\text{T}^{-2}\Theta^{-1}$. RHS: $\frac{[M]\cdot\text{L}^2\text{T}^{-2}\Theta^{-1}}{[M]} = \text{L}^2\text{T}^{-2}\Theta^{-1}$. Dimensionally consistent.</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>**Limitations:** Accuracy depends on how closely 'identical conditions' are met. Slight differences in surface emissivity, convection currents, or calorimeter heat capacity introduce error. Typically $\pm 5	ext{--}10\%$ error.</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>**Calorimeter water equivalent:** If the calorimeter cup absorbs significant heat, include its water equivalent $W$: $(m_1c_1 + Wc_w)/t_1 = (m_2c_2 + Wc_w)/t_2$. For thin copper calorimeters, $W$ is often negligible.</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>**Graphical method:** Plot $\ln(T - T_0)$ vs $t$ for both liquids. Slope $= -k$. If slopes match, $k$ is identical. Then compare intercepts or use area under curve for more precision.</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** Numerical problems give $m_1$, $c_1$, $m_2$, $t_1$, $t_2$ → find $c_2$. Also conceptual: 'Why must temperature range be small?' 'Why same calorimeter?' 'Why same volume?'</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>**Connection to Stefan-Boltzmann:** Newton's law is the linear approximation of radiative cooling $P = e\sigma A(T^4 - T_0^4)$ for small $\Delta T$: $T^4 - T_0^4 \approx 4T_0^3(T - T_0)$, giving $k \propto T_0^3$.</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>**Real-world application:** Quality control in food industry — comparing cooling rates of oils to detect adulteration. Engine coolant testing. Forensic estimation of time of death (body cooling).</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ Specific heat capacity is the same for all states of a substance. ✅ Ice ($c=2100$), liquid water ($c=4186$), and steam ($c=2010$) all have different specific heats. The value changes at phase transitions.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Specific heat depends on the shape/size of the object. ✅ Specific heat is an INTENSIVE property - it depends only on the material, not on the object's shape, size, or mass. A gold ring and a gold bar have the same $c$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ $c_p$ and $c_v$ are the same for all substances. ✅ For solids and liquids, $c_p \approx c_v$ because they barely expand. For gases, $c_p > c_v$ significantly because gases expand and do work. The difference is $c_p - c_v = R/M$ (per kg) or $R$ (per mole).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A substance with high specific heat is always "hotter." ✅ High $c$ means the substance RESISTS temperature change - it takes a lot of heat to raise its temperature. Water at 20°C has high $c$ but isn't "hot" - it just requires a lot of energy to change its temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The specific heat of water is 1 cal/g·°C everywhere. ✅ The calorie is DEFINED using water's specific heat, but that specific heat varies slightly with temperature. At 15°C, $c = 4.1855\text{ J/g·K}$ (giving 1 cal = 4.1855 J). The modern exact value is $1\text{ cal} = 4.184\text{ J}$ (thermochemical calorie).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Gases have only one specific heat capacity. ✅ Gases have TWO: $c_p$ (constant pressure) and $c_v$ (constant volume). $c_p - c_v = R$ per mole, or $c_p - c_v = R/M$ per unit mass. For air, $c_p = 1005$ and $c_v = 718\text{ J/kg·K}$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Metals always have low specific heat. ✅ While most metals do have low $c$ (because they're dense and atoms are heavy), beryllium ($c = 1825$) and lithium ($c = 3580$) are exceptions - they have low atomic mass, so per kg they have many atoms and high heat capacity.</td></tr><tr><td>Cooling rate ($dT/dt$) is the same as cooling constant ($k$) — Cooling rate changes with temperature; $k$ is constant for a given setup. The law says $dT/dt = -k(T-T_0)$, so rate depends on instantaneous temperature difference.</td></tr><tr><td>Any two liquids can be compared directly — They must be in the **same calorimeter**, same volume, same initial temperature, same surroundings. Different containers change surface area and $k$.</td></tr><tr><td>The method works for large temperature differences — Newton's law is valid only for small $\Delta T$ (typically $<30^\circ\text{C}$). For large $\Delta T$, radiation dominates and cooling is not exponential.</td></tr><tr><td>Specific heat of water is exactly $1\text{ cal/g·°C}$ at all temperatures — $c_w$ varies slightly with temperature ($4.186\text{ J/g·K}$ at $20^\circ\text{C}$, $4.219$ at $0^\circ\text{C}$). For school physics, $4.2\text{ J/g·K}$ or $1\text{ cal/g·°C}$ is standard.</td></tr><tr><td>Time $t$ is directly proportional to specific heat $c$ — From $c_2 = \frac{m_1c_1}{m_2}\frac{t_2}{t_1}$, if $m_1=m_2$ and $c_1$ known, $c_2 \propto t_2$. But $t$ is the time for a **fixed $\Delta T$**, not total cooling time.</td></tr><tr><td>Water equivalent $W$ is the same as mass of calorimeter — $W = m_{cal}c_{cal}/c_w$. For copper ($c_{cal} \approx 390\text{ J/kg·K}$), $W \approx 0.093\times m_{cal}$. Not equal to mass.</td></tr><tr><td>Method of cooling replaces method of mixtures — Method of mixtures is more accurate for solids. Method of cooling is specifically for **liquids** where mixing would cause confusion or reaction.</td></tr><tr><td>The formula $m_1c_1/t_1 = m_2c_2/t_2$ is exact — It assumes identical $k$, negligible calorimeter heat capacity, constant $c$ over the range, and no heat loss other than through the calorimeter walls. Real experiments have $\sim 5\%$ error.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids and liquids is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</p><hr><h2>Limitations of Newton's Law of Cooling</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Newton's law of cooling states: $\frac{dT}{dt} = -k(T - T_0)$, where $T$ is body temperature, $T_0$ is surrounding temperature, and $k$ is a positive constant. This law has several **critical limitations** that restrict its applicability.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Limitation 1 — Small temperature difference:** The law holds accurately only when $|T - T_0| < 30^\circ\text{C}$ (roughly). For larger differences, radiative heat loss dominates, which follows the **Stefan-Boltzmann law**: $P = e\sigma A(T^4 - T_0^4)$, not linear in $\Delta T$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>The linear approximation: $T^4 - T_0^4 = (T-T_0)(T+T_0)(T^2+T_0^2) \approx 4T_0^3(T-T_0)$ for small $\Delta T$. The approximation breaks down when $\Delta T$ is large — radiative cooling is much faster than predicted.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Limitation 2 — Constant surrounding temperature $T_0$:** $T_0$ must remain constant throughout cooling. If $T_0$ changes (e.g., cooling in a room that itself heats up), the differential equation changes, and $k$ is no longer constant.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Limitation 3 — Same medium throughout cooling:** The medium surrounding the body must be the same (same density, same composition). If the body moves between media (air to water), $k$ changes discontinuously.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Limitation 4 — No phase change:** The law assumes no latent heat effects. If the body undergoes phase change (water evaporating, ice melting), the temperature plateau absorbs/releases enormous heat, violating the single-$c$ assumption.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Limitation 5 — Uniform temperature throughout body:** Newton's law assumes the **lumped capacitance model** — internal thermal resistance is negligible, so $T$ is uniform throughout. This requires the **Biot number** $Bi = \frac{hL_c}{K} \ll 1$, where $L_c$ is characteristic length and $K$ thermal conductivity.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>For large objects (large $Bi$), the surface cools faster than the interior — temperature gradient exists. The body's effective cooling follows a sum of exponentials, not a single exponential. Newton's law describes only the first (dominant) mode.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Limitation 6 — No internal heat generation:** The law assumes no heat is produced inside the body. Endothermic reactions, electrical heating, or nuclear decay would add to the heat balance, invalidating the simple model.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Limitation 7 — Constant $k$:** The cooling constant $k = \frac{hA}{mc}$ depends on heat transfer coefficient $h$, surface area $A$, mass $m$, and specific heat $c$. $h$ itself depends on $T$, air velocity, and surface emissivity — not truly constant.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>For cooling in **still air**: $h \approx 5\text{--}25\text{ W/m}^2\text{K}$, dominated by natural convection. For **forced convection** (wind): $h$ can be $25\text{--}250\text{ W/m}^2\text{K}$, making $k$ velocity-dependent.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Mathematical derivation of limitations:** Starting from Stefan-Boltzmann: $\frac{dQ}{dt} = \epsilon\sigma A(T^4 - T_0^4)$. With $dQ = mc\,dT$: $\frac{dT}{dt} = \frac{\epsilon\sigma A}{mc}(T^4 - T_0^4)$. Linearization at $T \approx T_0$: $\frac{dT}{dt} = \frac{4\epsilon\sigma A T_0^3}{mc}(T-T_0) = -k(T-T_0)$ with $k = \frac{4\epsilon\sigma A T_0^3}{mc}$.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>The **correction factor** for large $\Delta T$: actual rate vs Newton prediction. For $\Delta T = 100^\circ\text{C}$ with $T_0 = 20^\circ\text{C}$, the radiative rate is ~3× the linear prediction. Error grows as $\sim 3T_0^2(\Delta T)^2$.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** State the limitations, explain why $T-T_0$ must be small, draw the comparison between Newton's law and Stefan's law graphs, calculate Biot number to check applicability.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Practical implications:** In **forensic science** (algor mortis), body cools from ~37°C to ambient (~20°C), $\Delta T \approx 17°C$ — within range. But for larger $\Delta T$ or outdoor exposure, corrections needed.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**When Newton's law IS valid:** Small $\Delta T$, still air, same medium, uniform $T$ inside body (small Bi), no phase change, no internal heat source. Laboratory cooling experiments with calorimeters are designed to satisfy these.</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>**Definition of water equivalent $W$:** The mass of water that would require the same amount of heat as a given body to raise its temperature by $1^\circ\text{C}$ (or 1 K). It is the **thermal capacity of the body expressed in mass units of water**.</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>**Thermal capacity (heat capacity):** $C = mc$ — the heat required to raise the temperature of a body by $1^\circ\text{C}$. Water equivalent $W$ is defined so that $W \times c_w = m \times c$, therefore **$W = \frac{m \cdot c}{c_w}$**, where $c_w = 4200\text{ J/kg·K}$ or $1\text{ cal/g·°C}$.</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>**Physical meaning:** A calorimeter with water equivalent $W = 20\text{ g}$ absorbs as much heat when warming by $1^\circ\text{C}$ as 20 g of water. The calorimeter's own mass $m_{cal}$ and its specific heat $c_{cal}$ contribute: $W = \frac{m_{cal} c_{cal}}{c_w}$.</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>**Derivation:** For a body of mass $m$, specific heat $c$, raising temperature by $\Delta T$: heat gained $H = mc\Delta T$. For $W$ grams of water gaining same heat: $H = W \cdot c_w \cdot \Delta T$. Equating: $mc\Delta T = W c_w \Delta T \Rightarrow W = \frac{mc}{c_w}$.</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>**Units:** Since $c$ has units $\text{J/kg·K}$ or $\text{cal/g·°C}$, and $m$ has kg or g, $W$ has units of **kg** or **g** (mass). Note: $W$ is not a heat capacity itself — it is a mass equivalent.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>**For calorimeter:** $W_{cal} = \frac{m_{cal} c_{cal}}{c_w}$. For thin copper calorimeter: $c_{Cu} \approx 390\text{ J/kg·K}$, $c_w = 4186\text{ J/kg·K}$, so $W_{Cu} \approx 0.093 m_{Cu}$. A 50 g copper calorimeter has $W \approx 4.7\text{ g}$.</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>**Why water as reference?** Water has the highest specific heat of common liquids ($c_w = 4186\text{ J/kg·K}$ at $20^\circ\text{C}$). Using water as the reference makes $W$ directly comparable to actual water mass — physical meaning is intuitive.</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>**Effective heat capacity in calorimetry:** When a calorimeter contains liquid of mass $m_l$, specific heat $c_l$, and water equivalent $W$, the total effective heat capacity is $C_{eff} = m_l c_l + W c_w$. This is used in both method of mixtures and method of cooling.</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>**In method of cooling:** Formula $(m_1 c_1 + W c_w)/t_1 = (m_2 c_2 + W c_w)/t_2$ accounts for calorimeter heat capacity. If $W$ is neglected, calculated $c_2$ has systematic error.</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>**Finding $W$ experimentally:** (1) Cool known mass of water from $T_1$ to $T_2$, measure time $t_1$. (2) Cool same calorimeter with different mass of water, measure $t_2$. (3) Solve two equations: $(m_1 c_w + W c_w)/t_1 = (m_2 c_w + W c_w)/t_2$. This eliminates $k$ and gives $W$.</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>**Dimensional analysis:** $[W] = [M]$. $[mc/c_w] = \frac{[M]\cdot[Q][\Theta]^{-1}}{[Q][\Theta]^{-1}} = [M]$. Dimensionally consistent.</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>**NEB exam focus:** Define $W$, derive $W = mc/c_w$, calculate $W$ for given calorimeter, include $W$ in calorimetry equations, distinguish between water equivalent and actual mass of calorimeter.</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>**Connection to heat capacity:** Heat capacity $C = mc$. Since $c_w = 1\text{ cal/g·°C}$ in CGS, $W = mc$ numerically equals the heat capacity in calories per degree. For any body in SI: $C = W \times 4.2\text{ J/°C}$.</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>**Applications:** Calorimetry (all types),热水器 design ($W$ concept used in thermal mass calculations), building thermal mass ($W$ of walls/furniture affects room temperature stability), spacecraft thermal control.</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>**Special cases:** For ideal gas in bomb calorimeter (constant volume): $C_V$ used instead of $c$. For phase change calorimetry: effective $W$ includes latent heat contributions. For food calorimetry: $W$ of bomb calorimeter includes ignition wire, crucible.</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>The **method of cooling experiment** measures specific heat capacity by comparing the time taken for two liquids to cool through the same temperature range under identical conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>Newton's law: $\frac{dT}{dt} = -k(T - T_0)$. For a body of mass $m$ and specific heat $c$, heat loss rate is $\frac{dQ}{dt} = mc\frac{dT}{dt} = -mc k(T - T_0)$.</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>In the lab, we **cannot measure $dT/dt$ continuously** with a stopwatch. Instead, we measure the **time $t$** for temperature to fall through a fixed interval $\Delta T$ (e.g., $60^\circ\text{C} \to 50^\circ\text{C}$). Average cooling rate $\approx \frac{\Delta T}{t}$.</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>For two liquids in the **same calorimeter** (same $k$, same surface area, same surroundings), the heat loss rates are equal at the same temperature: $m_1c_1\frac{\Delta T}{t_1} = m_2c_2\frac{\Delta T}{t_2}$.</td></tr><tr><td>36</td><td>Since $\Delta T$ is identical, it cancels out, giving the practical formula: **$\frac{m_1c_1}{t_1} = \frac{m_2c_2}{t_2}$** or **$c_2 = \frac{m_1c_1}{m_2}\frac{t_2}{t_1}$**.</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>**Apparatus:** Calorimeter (thin copper cup) with stirrer and lid, thermometer ($0	ext{--}100^\circ\text{C}$, least count $0.5^\circ\text{C}$ or $0.1^\circ\text{C}$), stopwatch, water (reference liquid), unknown liquid, burner/heater, stand.</td></tr><tr><td>38</td><td>**Procedure:** (1) Weigh empty calorimeter $\to m_{cal}$. (2) Fill with known volume of water $\to$ find mass $m_w$. (3) Heat to $\sim 70^\circ\text{C}$. (4) Place in constant-temperature enclosure (or still air). (5) Stir gently, record temperature every 30 s or 1 min. (6) Note time $t_w$ for $60^\circ\text{C} \to 50^\circ\text{C}$. (7) Repeat with same volume of unknown liquid $\to m_l$, $t_l$. (8) Calculate $c_l$.</td></tr><tr><td>39</td><td>**Why same volume?** Same volume $\to$ same liquid height $\to$ same surface area exposed $\to$ same $k$. If masses differ due to density, use $m_1$, $m_2$ in formula.</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>**Stirring:** Essential to maintain uniform temperature throughout liquid. Without stirring, surface cools faster than bulk $\to$ non-uniform $T$ $\to$ Newton's law invalid.</td></tr><tr><td>41</td><td>**Surroundings:** Must be constant temperature. Best done in a **double-walled vessel** (water jacket) or a large room with stable temperature. Drafts cause forced convection $\to$ $k$ changes.</td></tr><tr><td>42</td><td>**Temperature range:** Choose range where $T - T_0 < 30^\circ\text{C}$ (e.g., $60\to50$, $50\to40$ if $T_0 \approx 25^\circ\text{C}$). Ensures linear approximation of radiation holds.</td></tr><tr><td>43</td><td>**Water equivalent of calorimeter:** If calorimeter absorbs significant heat, effective thermal capacity is $(m_l c_l + W c_w)$. Formula becomes $\frac{m_w c_w + W c_w}{t_w} = \frac{m_l c_l + W c_w}{t_l}$. $W$ found by separate experiment (cooling empty calorimeter + water).</td></tr><tr><td>44</td><td>**Graphical analysis:** Plot $T$ vs $t$ for both liquids. Draw tangents at same $T$ to find $(dT/dt)_1$, $(dT/dt)_2$. Or plot $\ln(T-T_0)$ vs $t$ — slope $= -k$. Verify $k$ matches for both runs.</td></tr><tr><td>45</td><td>**Sources of error:** (1) Heat loss by radiation not purely Newtonian, (2) Convection currents in room, (3) Thermometer lag, (4) Incomplete stirring, (5) Evaporation (especially volatile liquids), (6) Calorimeter $W$ not accounted, (7) Temperature range too large.</td></tr><tr><td>46</td><td>**Accuracy:** Typically $\pm 5	ext{--}10\%$. For better accuracy: use oil bath for constant $T_0$, electrical heating for precise initial $T$, digital thermometer with data logging.</td></tr><tr><td>47</td><td>**NEB practical exam:** Common questions: 'Describe the method of cooling experiment.' 'Why same calorimeter?' 'Why stir?' 'Why small temperature range?' 'How to find water equivalent?' 'Plot cooling curve and find $k$.'</td></tr><tr><td>48</td><td>**Alternative: Continuous cooling curve.** Record $T(t)$ for both liquids. Fit $T(t) = T_0 + (T_i - T_0)e^{-kt}$. Extract $k$ and $T_i$. If $k$ identical, ratio of initial slopes gives $c$ ratio.</td></tr><tr><td>49</td><td>**Purpose of Newton's law of cooling:** The law provides a simple mathematical model to predict how the temperature of a body changes with time when it is placed in a surroundings of constant temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>50</td><td>**Forensic science — estimating time of death:** A corpse cools according to Newton's law after death (the "algor mortis" process). If body temperature is measured at time $t$, and the ambient temperature is known, the law estimates how long ago death occurred: $\theta(t) = \theta_0 + (\theta_{body} - \theta_0)e^{-K't}$.</td></tr><tr><td>51</td><td>**Engineering — cooling system design:** Engineers use Newton's law to size radiators, heat sinks, and cooling fans. For a component dissipating power $P$, the steady-state temperature rise is $\Delta T = P/(kA)$, where $k$ is the heat transfer coefficient.</td></tr><tr><td>52</td><td>**Food industry — pasteurization and chilling:** After pasteurization, milk must be cooled to below 4°C within a specified time to prevent bacterial growth. Newton's law calculates the required cooling time and the size of the heat exchanger.</td></tr><tr><td>53</td><td>**Everyday life — coffee and tea:** A hot cup of coffee cools faster when the room is cold and slower when the room is warm. The law explains why adding cold milk immediately (rather than waiting) keeps coffee hotter longer — the milk lowers the initial temperature, reducing the rate of heat loss.</td></tr><tr><td>54</td><td>**Metallurgy — quenching and annealing:** The rate of cooling a hot metal determines its microstructure and hardness. Quenching in water (large $k$) produces hard martensite; slow cooling in oil or air (smaller $k$) produces softer phases. Newton's law approximates the cooling curve.</td></tr><tr><td>55</td><td>**Climate and building science — thermal mass:** Walls and floors with high heat capacity (large $mc$) change temperature slowly. Newton's law predicts how quickly a building cools after the heating is turned off, helping design energy-efficient HVAC systems.</td></tr><tr><td>56</td><td>**Weather forecasting — radiation fog:** On clear nights, the ground loses heat by radiation. Newton's law approximates how quickly surface temperature drops. When it falls below the dew point, fog forms. This is used in agricultural frost warnings.</td></tr><tr><td>57</td><td>**Electronic devices — CPU thermal management:** A processor generates heat at rate $P$. Without cooling, its temperature rises until heat loss balances power input. With a heatsink, Newton's law predicts the equilibrium temperature: $T_{eq} = T_{ambient} + P/k$.</td></tr><tr><td>58</td><td>**Calorimetry corrections — radiation loss:** In precision calorimetry, heat loss to surroundings causes systematic errors. Newton's law allows correction: the heat lost during an experiment is $\int k(\theta - \theta_0)\,dt$, which can be estimated from the cooling curve.</td></tr><tr><td>59</td><td>**Medicine — therapeutic hypothermia:** After cardiac arrest, patients are cooled to 32–34°C to reduce brain damage. Newton's law guides the cooling protocol, predicting how long ice packs or cooling blankets must be applied.</td></tr><tr><td>60</td><td>**Photography — darkroom processing:** Chemical baths in photography must be kept at a precise temperature. Newton's law predicts how quickly a fresh bath reaches the target temperature when moved from a warm storage area.</td></tr><tr><td>61</td><td>**Automotive — engine warm-up and cooling:** A car engine reaches operating temperature faster in cold weather (larger $\theta - \theta_0$) but the cooling system must dissipate more heat. Newton's law models both warm-up and cooling phases.</td></tr><tr><td>62</td><td>**NEB exam applications:** Identify real-world situations where Newton's law applies. Explain how the law is used in forensic time-of-death estimation and in finding unknown specific heats by the method of cooling.</td></tr><tr><td>63</td><td>**Limitation reminder:** Newton's law is an approximation. Real cooling involves radiation ($\propto T^4 - T_0^4$), convection, and conduction simultaneously. The law is most accurate when temperature differences are moderate (20–50°C) and convection dominates.</td></tr><tr><td>64</td><td>**Fact.** 1st law (inertia): a body stays in uniform motion unless acted on by a net external force.</td></tr><tr><td>65</td><td>**Fact.** 2nd law: net force = rate of change of momentum = m·a (for constant m).</td></tr><tr><td>66</td><td>**Fact.** 3rd law: forces are mutual, equal and opposite, acting on different bodies (never cancel on the same body).</td></tr><tr><td>67</td><td>**Fact.** Impulse = ∫F dt = Δp; F = ma gives F = m·(Δv/Δt).</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>The law fails at large temperature differences: radiation then dominates and the body's temperature follows the T⁴ Stefan law, not a straight line.</td></tr><tr><td>It also fails when the surroundings are not at a fixed temperature, when cooling is forced (fan, wind), or when the body changes phase while cooling.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Newton's law of cooling works for all temperature differences — It is valid only for $|T - T_0| < 30^\circ\text{C}$ (approx). For large differences, radiative cooling dominates and follows $T^4 - T_0^4$, giving faster cooling than the linear law predicts.</td></tr><tr><td>The cooling constant $k$ is always the same — $k = hA/(mc)$ depends on heat transfer coefficient $h$ which varies with temperature, air velocity, and surface conditions. $k$ is only approximately constant within the small $\Delta T$ range.</td></tr><tr><td>A large body cools the same way as a small body — For large bodies, **Biot number $Bi = hL_c/K$** determines if lumped capacitance applies. Large $Bi$ (metals, large solids) → significant temperature gradient inside body → Newton's law fails.</td></tr><tr><td>Surrounding temperature $T_0$ can change during cooling — $T_0$ must be **constant**. If surroundings heat up (sun, nearby heater), or if the body itself warms the air around it, $k$ is not constant and the exponential solution $T(t) = T_0 + (T_i-T_0)e^{-kt}$ is invalid.</td></tr><tr><td>The formula $T(t) = T_0 + (T_i - T_0)e^{-kt}$ works for any body — This solution holds only when $k$ is constant (small $\Delta T$) and no phase change/internal heat. For radiation-dominated cooling, $T(t)$ follows a different integral.</td></tr><tr><td>Evaporation doesn't affect cooling in a covered calorimeter — Evaporation removes heat (latent heat $\approx 2260\text{ kJ/kg}$) and changes liquid composition (concentration). Even in covered calorimeters, some evaporation occurs. For volatile liquids, error is significant.</td></tr><tr><td>Newton's law applies to all states of matter — Valid for liquids and gases where convection homogenizes temperature. For solids without internal convection, the **Fourier heat equation** applies, not Newton's law.</td></tr><tr><td>If two bodies have the same mass and same $c$, they cool identically — Surface area $A$ is in $k$. Two spheres of same mass but different radii have different $A/m$ ratio → different $k$ → different cooling rates. Shape matters.</td></tr><tr><td>The lumped capacitance assumption (uniform body temperature) is always valid for liquids — For liquids in still air, convection currents maintain uniformity. But in a viscous liquid cooling by conduction alone, internal gradients exist and Newton's law over-simplifies.</td></tr><tr><td>The law fails only at very high temperatures — The failure depends on **$\Delta T$ relative to $T_0$**, not absolute $T$. A body at $100^\circ\text{C}$ cooling in $25^\circ\text{C}$ air ($\Delta T = 75^\circ\text{C}$) fails the linear approximation even though absolute $T$ is not 'high'.</td></tr><tr><td>Water equivalent $W$ is the mass of water in the calorimeter — $W$ is a **calculated mass-equivalent**, not actual water present. For a dry copper calorimeter of mass 50 g, $W \approx 4.7\text{ g}$. No water is inside.</td></tr><tr><td>Water equivalent and heat capacity are the same thing — Heat capacity $C = mc$ has units $\text{J/°C}$. Water equivalent $W = mc/c_w$ has units of **mass** (g or kg). They are related by $C = W \times c_w$, but numerically different in SI.</td></tr><tr><td>The water equivalent of a calorimeter is constant — $W = m_{cal} c_{cal}/c_w$. Since $c_w$ is nearly constant, $W$ is constant for a given calorimeter. But $c_w$ itself varies with temperature ($4.186\to4.219\text{ J/g·K}$), so $W$ varies slightly.</td></tr><tr><td>A heavier calorimeter always has larger $W$ — Not always. $W \propto m \times c_{cal}$. Aluminum ($c \approx 900$) has higher specific heat per unit mass than copper ($c \approx 390$). So an aluminum calorimeter has **higher $W$ per unit mass** than copper.</td></tr><tr><td>Water equivalent is used only in method of cooling — $W$ appears in **all calorimetry calculations**: method of mixtures, method of cooling, bomb calorimetry, and steam calorimetry. Any time a calorimeter absorbs heat, $W$ must be included.</td></tr><tr><td>The formula $W = mc/c_w$ means the body behaves like $mc/c_w$ grams of water — **Yes, this is exactly the physical meaning.** A 100 g copper block ($c = 390$) has $W = 100\times390/4186 = 9.3\text{ g}$ — it behaves thermally like 9.3 g of water.</td></tr><tr><td>Water equivalent can be negative — $W$ is always positive since $m > 0$, $c > 0$, $c_w > 0$. However, in some contexts (endothermic reactions in calorimeter) the effective $W$ from calibration includes correction factors.</td></tr><tr><td>All calorimeters have negligible $W$ — Thin copper calorimeters have small $W \approx 0.1 m$. But thick brass calorimeters, massive aluminum blocks, or calorimeters with large stirrers can have $W$ comparable to liquid mass ($\sim 20\%\text{--}50\%$ of liquid mass).</td></tr><tr><td>Water equivalent is independent of temperature — $c_w$ varies by $\sim 1\%$ from $0^\circ\text{C}$ to $100^\circ\text{C}$. For precise work, use $c_w$ at the **average temperature** of the experiment. In school physics, $4.2\text{ J/g·K}$ is standard.</td></tr><tr><td>Water equivalent is measured in liters — Since density of water $\approx 1\text{ g/mL}$, $1000\text{ g} = 1\text{ L}$. But $W$ is defined in **mass units**, not volume. A $W = 50\text{ g}$ means thermal capacity equivalent to 50 mL of water.</td></tr><tr><td>We measure cooling rate $dT/dt$ directly with a stopwatch — We measure **time $t$ for a fixed $\Delta T$**, then average rate $= \Delta T/t$. Instantaneous $dT/dt$ requires continuous recording or tangent method.</td></tr><tr><td>The formula $m_1c_1/t_1 = m_2c_2/t_2$ works for any temperature range — Only valid for **small $\Delta T$** where Newton's law holds ($T - T_0 < 30^\circ\text{C}$). Large range $\to$ $k$ not constant $\to$ formula fails.</td></tr><tr><td>Stirring is optional — **Stirring is mandatory**. Without it, temperature is not uniform, $T$ in Newton's law is undefined (surface vs bulk), and cooling is non-exponential.</td></tr><tr><td>Water equivalent $W$ is negligible for all calorimeters — For **thin copper** cups, $W \approx 0.1 m_{cal}$ may be small. For thick brass/aluminum, $W$ is significant. Always check if given in problem.</td></tr><tr><td>The method works for any two liquids — Liquids must **not react** with calorimeter, **not evaporate significantly**, and have **similar viscosity** (affects convection). Mercury, acids, volatile organics are problematic.</td></tr><tr><td>Initial temperature must be exactly the same for both runs — **Temperature range** (e.g., $60\to50$) must be same. Initial $T_i$ can differ slightly if $T_0$ constant, but same $T_i$ ensures identical $k(T-T_0)$ profile.</td></tr><tr><td>Cooling time $t$ is inversely proportional to specific heat $c$ — From $c_2 = \frac{m_1c_1}{m_2}\frac{t_2}{t_1}$, if $m_1=m_2$, $c_2 \propto t_2$. But this is for **fixed $\Delta T$**, not total cooling time to room temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>The calorimeter's water equivalent is the same as its mass — $W = m_{cal} c_{cal} / c_w$. For copper ($c_{cal} \approx 390\text{ J/kg·K}$), $W \approx 0.093 m_{cal}$. For aluminum ($c_{cal} \approx 900$), $W \approx 0.21 m_{cal}$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Newton's law of cooling tells you the exact time of death. ✅ It gives an ESTIMATE. The model ignores many factors (clothing, body size, air movement, humidity) that affect real cooling rates.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The law works equally well for very large temperature differences (e.g., a red-hot iron in air). ✅ For large $\Delta T$, radiation ($\propto T^4$) dominates over convection, and Newton's linear law becomes inaccurate.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A larger object always cools faster. ✅ Larger objects have larger heat capacity ($mc$), which slows cooling. The cooling RATE depends on the ratio $k/(mc)$, not just on size.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Newton's law of cooling is the same as the Stefan-Boltzmann law. ✅ Newton's law is linear ($d\theta/dt \propto \theta - \theta_0$). Stefan-Boltzmann is quartic ($\propto T^4 - T_0^4$). Newton's law is an approximation valid for small $\Delta T$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The cooling constant $k$ is a property of the material. ✅ $k$ depends on the SURFACE properties (area, texture, air flow), not the material inside. Two objects of the same material but different shapes have different $k$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Once a body reaches room temperature, it stays there forever. ✅ It reaches thermal equilibrium asymptotically — it gets arbitrarily close but never exactly reaches $\theta_0$ in finite time (though for practical purposes it appears to).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The method of cooling (comparing cooling rates) gives the exact specific heat. ✅ It gives an approximate value. Errors arise from non-identical conditions, radiation losses, and the approximation that $k$ is constant.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ 1st law (inertia): a body stays in uniform motion unless acted on by a net external force..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Newton's law of cooling is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics. Water Equivalent (W) — Definition & Derivation covers essential principles and applications. Measuring Cooling Rate in the Lab (Time-Based 'Method of Cooling') covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Heat (H) - Mass and Temperature Dependency</td><td>Heat (H) — Mass and Temperature Dependency covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Newton's Law of Cooling</td><td>Newton's Law of Cooling is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids and liquids</td><td>Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids and liquids (Quantity of Heat, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids, liquids. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Specific Heat Capacity</td><td>Specific Heat Capacity is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics. Specific Heat Capacity (c) — Definition & Key Values covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Change of Phases and Latent Heat</td><td>Change of Phases and Latent Heat is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Heat Capacity (Thermal Capacity, C')</td><td>Heat Capacity (Thermal Capacity, C′) covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Specific latent heat of fusion and vaporization</td><td>Specific latent heat of fusion and vaporization (Quantity of Heat, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Specific latent heat of fusion, vaporization. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Triple Point</td><td>The triple point is the unique P–T state where solid, liquid and vapour coexist. Water’s value is about 273.16 K and 611.7 Pa.</td></tr><tr><td>9: Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion and vaporiz…</td><td>Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion and vaporiz… (Quantity of Heat, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion, vaporization. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Principle of Calorimetry (Method of Mixtures)</td><td>Principle of Calorimetry (Method of Mixtures) covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>11: Newton's Law of Cooling - Statement</td><td>Newton's Law of Cooling — Statement covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>12: Triple point</td><td>Triple point (Quantity of Heat, Physics, class-11-notes) covers Triple point. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these. Triple Point covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>13: Finding an Unknown Specific Heat Capacity by Comparing Cooling Rates</td><td>Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids and liquids is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics.</td></tr><tr><td>14: Limitations of Newton's Law of Cooling</td><td>Newton's law of cooling is a fundamental concept in Science that covers the essential principles, formulas, and applications. Understanding this topic is crucial for solving related problems and building foundation for advanced topics. Water Equivalent (W) — Definition & Derivation covers essential principles and applications. Measuring Cooling Rate in the Lab (Time-Based 'Method of Cooling') covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Quantity Of Heat.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Energy accounting depends on a stated sign convention. With heat added to a system positive and work done by the system positive, the first law is ΔU = Q − W. Heat and work describe transfers; internal energy is a state property.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>The ideal-gas equation is a model: particles are treated as small and far apart, with negligible intermolecular forces except during collisions. It is most reliable at sufficiently low density and away from condensation; real gases depart from it at high pressure or low temperature.</p><h3>Open research question — The thermodynamic arrow of time</h3><p>Macroscopic heat flow has a preferred direction, while many microscopic laws work nearly the same forward and backward in time. How the universe’s low-entropy past underwrites that observed arrow remains a foundational research question; it does not invalidate the second law’s successful predictions.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>For “Quantity Of Heat”, identify one assumption in the model, explain what observation would test it, and state what evidence could distinguish the accepted account from the open question “The thermodynamic arrow of time”.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/13-3-the-ideal-gas-law">OpenStax Physics: ideal-gas model</a></li><li><a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/time-thermo/">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy: thermodynamic asymmetry</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>